  "dom_id": "step-21027",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "The graph of any cubic function $y = f(x)= ax^3 + bx^2 + cx +d,$ where $a >0,$ has a characteristic inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape, with $y$-values ranging from $-\\infty$ to $+\\infty.$ This means that every such function has at least one real root and at most three distinct roots. \n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to plot the graph of $y = x^3-3x^2 +3x-1.$ First, we need to find the roots, so we set $y=0$ and solve. Noting that the polynomial is a perfect cube, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get one real root $x=1,$ which has multiplicity $3.$ \n\nAt a root of multiplicity $3,$ the graph becomes **tangent** (i.e., lies parallel) to the $x$-axis and crosses the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240558/assets/d87834ef3f7225b00d92e6051a67735cf6695c5a2eff9635c4de416d0be1577f.png)",
